(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 8, part: `Part Three: One Chart`, title: `The Blueprint`, weeks: `Months 7 to 9`,
  tasks: [`SB4`,`SB6`,`SB3`,`SB5`,`SB1`,`E5`,`E7`,`E6`,`C6`,`L3`],
  cast: [
    [`Irene Kowalski`,`Retired nurse manager, Greeley ICU. Charge nurse on nights when Maria started, and the one who pushed the manager to hire her. Wrote the unit's first downtime binder.`],
    [`Asha Shah`,`Retired ER charge nurse. Ran nights in the Greeley ER for years, one floor down from Irene's ICU.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Friday, May 14, Tributary answered Nate's question in one paragraph. Its disaster recovery plan would get its first full test in January, as part of a new yearly program, and Cascade was welcome to observe. One Chart went live in October.`,
    `Nate called before Maria had finished reading it. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"So the first time anybody finds out if it works,"</span> he said, <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"is three months after three hospitals depend on it."</span>`,
    `The design phase had started that week. By the end of July, every decision about how One Chart would run in Wyoming had to be on paper and signed: how it would recover, what its data meant, what moved and what didn't. Corvane's build team would be in Cheyenne in June. Kiran had left at 5:10 every night that week. Maria had checked.`,
    `At 9:14 that night, on the couch, her phone buzzed.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Theo Reyes" time="9:14 PM">Had lunch with Irene in Greeley last week. She still asks about you. Every single time. I'm sending you her number so you can't tell me you lost it. Call her.</prop>`,
    `Maria hadn't lost it. She'd had Irene's number for eighteen years. She'd left Irene's retirement party early in 2022 for a meeting she couldn't remember now, and she'd meant to call the next week, and then it had been four years. Theo had been her partner on nights for ten of them. He was the only person who could have sent that text.`,
    `She called at 9:31, before she could think of a reason not to. It rang four times. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Maria Santos,"</span> Irene said, the way she used to read a name off the census board. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Theo said you might. Tomorrow at ten. You remember the house."</span> She hung up before Maria could apologize, which was also how she'd run report.`,
    `Lucia had heard every word from the other end of the couch. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Who's Irene?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"The woman who got me hired."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"You sound scared of her."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Everybody was,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"That's how you knew she was good."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: how long, and how much`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Two questions`,
        body: [`Ochoa drew a clock on the legal pad at the Greeley diner and cut it in half with one line. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Two questions,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"How long can you be down? And how much can you lose?"</span>`,
          `Business continuity keeps the work going while a system is down: downtime procedures, paper, people. Disaster recovery brings the systems back. Both start with a business impact analysis. For each system, ask the people who depend on it what happens to patients and operations at fifteen minutes down, at an hour, at a day. From those answers the system's owner sets two targets. The recovery time objective, RTO, is how fast it has to be running again. The recovery point objective, RPO, is the most data you can afford to lose, counted back from the failure. Backups every four hours mean you could lose four hours. IT designs to meet the targets. It doesn't pick them.`],
        terms: [[`Business impact analysis`,`An assessment of what an outage of each system costs patients and operations over time, used to set recovery targets.`],[`RTO`,`Recovery time objective. The longest a system can be down before it must be running again.`],[`RPO`,`Recovery point objective. The most data, measured in time, that can be lost when a system fails.`]],
        exam: `RTO is time to restore. RPO is data you can lose. Owners set both through a business impact analysis, and IT designs to meet them. Tighter targets cost more, so they go where patients need them.` },
      { h: `Hot, warm, cold`,
        body: [`A hot site is fully equipped, with current data, and takes over in minutes to hours. A mirrored site goes further: a live copy with automatic failover, so almost nothing is lost. A warm site is partly equipped, with some hardware and network, and you restore data from backup, so it's back in hours to days. A cold site is space, power, and network only, and takes days to weeks. Keep backups everywhere, offline or unchangeable, so ransomware can't reach them. Give each system the cheapest design that meets both its targets.`,
          `Then look for single points of failure in the infrastructure around it: one network circuit, one power feed, one engineer. Redundancy only helps if the two paths can't fail together, so a second circuit in the same trench isn't a second circuit. Availability is the share of time a service is up. At 99.9 percent, a 30-day month allows 43.2 minutes down: 0.001 times 43,200 minutes.`],
        terms: [[`Hot site`,`A fully equipped recovery site with current data, ready in minutes to hours. A mirrored site adds a live copy and automatic failover.`],[`Warm site`,`A partly equipped recovery site. Data is restored from backup, and it's back in hours to days.`],[`Cold site`,`Space, power, and network only. Equipment and data are brought in, over days to weeks.`]],
        exam: `Expect to pick a site type from an RTO and RPO, and to turn an availability percentage into minutes of downtime. Redundant infrastructure only counts when the paths are independent.` },
      { h: `Who owns the meaning`,
        body: [`Data governance decides what data means, who may use it, and how good it has to be. The data owner is a business leader, accountable for it: the CNOs for nursing data, the lab director for results. The data steward keeps it right day to day, maintains its definitions in the data dictionary, and fixes quality problems. The data custodian, usually IT or a vendor, stores it, backs it up, and applies the access the owner approved. A governance council settles what owners can't settle alone.`,
          `A conversion tests all of it. The master patient index, the MPI, links every record to one person. A duplicate splits a patient's history across two charts. An overlay, two people merged into one, is worse. Link only sure matches automatically, have trained HIM staff review the uncertain middle, and measure the duplicate rate before and after.`],
        terms: [[`Data steward`,`The person who maintains a data element's definition and quality for its owner.`],[`Data dictionary`,`The record of each data element's name, definition, owner, format, and history.`],[`Master patient index`,`The index that links all of a person's records to one identity across systems.`]],
        exam: `The owner decides, the steward maintains, the custodian protects. If a question asks who defines a data element, the answer is the business owner through governance, never the analyst who maps it.` },
      { h: `New things, on purpose`,
        body: [`New technology shows up from every direction: a vendor's pitch, a doctor back from a conference, a board member's article. Bring it in through one process. Watch the trends, take every request through a single intake, weigh it against the strategy, pilot what passes with measures set in advance, then scale it or stop it.`,
          `Evaluating an emerging technology means hard questions. What's the evidence, in patients like yours? If it's software that informs care, is the FDA's oversight of medical devices in play, and is it cleared for this use? Does it connect through standards like FHIR? Who acts on what it finds at 2 AM? Does it work where your patients live? What does it cost after the free year?`,
          `The trends worth that work reach patients: telehealth and remote ICU monitoring, portals and patient apps on FHIR APIs, monitoring at home, population health. Then forecast what they'll need. Build capacity and skills forecasts from their drivers, like volumes, new services, and users, and redo them every year.`],
        terms: [[`Technology intake`,`One governed path for requests and trends: screen, evaluate, pilot with measures, then scale or stop.`],[`FHIR API`,`A standard web interface that lets apps, including patients' own apps, read and exchange health data.`]],
        exam: `For incorporating trends, the answer is a governed process, not a quick yes or a freeze. For a new technology, evidence, regulatory status, interoperability, workflow, and total cost beat a good demo. Forecasts come from drivers, not last year plus ten percent.` },
      { h: `What comes across`,
        body: [`Before a cutover, inventory every application touching the old system, by family: clinical, administrative, financial, consumer, business intelligence. Give each one an owner and a fate: move, replace, retire, or reconnect. The ones nobody listed break first.`,
          `Clinical content needs the same care. Every order set and decision support rule gets a clinical owner, a review date, a version number, and usage data, through the clinical informatics council. Medical devices need biomed, nursing, pharmacy, and IT together: a separate device network, a drug library pharmacy owns, and validation before anyone relies on it.`],
        terms: [[`Medical device integration`,`Moving data between bedside devices, like pumps and monitors, and the EHR.`]],
        exam: `Expect to name an application's family, to keep clinical content current through clinical owners and review cycles, and to integrate devices with clinical engineering on a segmented network.` },
      { h: `Irene's page`,
        body: [`Ochoa left the bottom of the last page blank. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Somebody else should write this part,"</span> he said. Irene Kowalski wrote it a week later, in blue ink, from the downtime binder she started for the Greeley ICU in 2007.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Write the downtime procedures before you need them, with the people who'll use them. Keep the paper forms on every unit, unlocked. Put a downtime computer on each unit, on battery, with its own printer and a read-only copy of the census, the meds, the allergies, and the latest results."</span>`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Practice it every quarter, like a mock code. When the system comes back, somebody enters what happened on paper and checks what came across against it. Count the pages."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Read-only downtime view`,`A local copy of critical patient information, refreshed on a schedule, that clinicians can read when the main system is down.`]],
        exam: `Downtime procedures exist before go-live, get practiced, and include recovery: entering what happened on paper and reconciling it. A recovery plan isn't proven until it's been tested.` }
    ],
    video: [`6mZe-VsIoxY`,`RTO, WRT, MTD & RPO Disaster Recovery Metrics: Essential CISSP 2026 Cybersecurity Concepts Explained`,`Gagan (Gags) Singh CISSP`,`8:30`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f8s1`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB4`, title:`Irene's table`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, May 15, Irene Kowalski's house was where it had always been, a brick ranch on a corner in west Greeley with a crab apple tree in the front yard. Maria sat in the driveway with the engine off for six minutes by the dashboard clock. Then the front door opened. A woman with short gray hair came down the walk jingling her keys, spotted the car, and changed course.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Well, I'll be,"</span> the woman said, and tapped on the glass until Maria rolled it down. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Irene's new grad. You used to come down to my ER at four in the morning for the good coffee."</span> It took Maria a second. Asha Shah had run nights in the Greeley ER for most of the years Maria was learning where it was. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"She's been watching for you since nine. Go on in."</span> Then she was in her car and gone.`,
        `Irene was at the kitchen table with two mugs already poured. She was smaller than Maria remembered. Her eyes weren't. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Santos."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Four years. I'm sorry. I left your party early, and then I never called."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"You're here now. Sit down before it gets cold."</span>`,
        `So Maria sat, and because it was easier, she started with work. Tributary. Forty good pages and a failover nobody had ever run. A first real test in January, three months after three hospitals would depend on it. Irene listened the way she used to listen to report, without interrupting, eyes on Maria's face. Then she got up, went to the hall closet, and came back with a binder gone soft at the corners. GREELEY ICU DOWNTIME, in marker, and under it, 2007.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Irene">"My second year, I lost a man because the paper forms were in a locked closet and nobody had the key,"</span> she said, and that was all she ever said about it. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"So after the long one in '07, I wrote this, and we practiced it every quarter whether anybody liked it or not. You run the mock code before anybody codes."</span>`,
        `Maria turned the pages. Paper order sheets. A census grid. A call tree full of numbers that had been dead for a decade. Near the back, on different paper, was a page Irene had added later.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Transfers by fax">Count the pages against the cover sheet. If the cover sheet is missing, or the count is wrong, call the sending hospital before the first dose. No exceptions on nights. I.K.</prop>`,
        `There was no date on it. It didn't need one. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"You wrote this after me."</span> Irene didn't pretend not to understand. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"After us."</span>`,
        `In fourteen years on the same unit, neither of them had ever said it out loud. Now it came out in pieces, the way report comes out at shift change, each of them filling in what the other skipped. A man sent from a small hospital in Wyoming, straight up to the ICU in the middle of the night, no stop in the ER. The fax machine at the charge desk. Irene pulling the sheet off the machine and putting it in Maria's hand. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Here's his list."</span> One page. It ended near the bottom, and it looked finished.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"There was a page two."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"There was a page two."</span> The anticoagulant he took at home had been on it. Maria had been minutes from starting him on another one. She'd caught it, and in eighteen years she'd never found a better word for how than luck. She'd hated the word the whole time.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Irene">"I handed it to you,"</span> Irene said. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"I was charge. Never looked for a cover sheet. Gave a new grad a sick man and half a list and went back to my desk."</span> She turned her mug a quarter turn on the table. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"I still think about that page every time I hear a fax machine. Which isn't often anymore, thank God."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"All these years I figured you were being nice to me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"I never mentioned it because it was mine."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It was mine, Irene."</span> Irene put her hand flat on the binder. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Then it's ours. And we've each been hauling it around alone for eighteen years, like a pair of mules. Couple of geniuses."</span> Maria laughed, wet and too loud, and Irene pushed the tissues at her without looking, the same way she used to toss a warm blanket across the charge desk at 4 AM.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Theo tells me your girl's coming to Greeley for nursing school."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"August. She wants the real story. I keep saying not tonight."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Then quit saying it. Tell her the whole thing. Not the version for committees. The part about luck, too."</span> She slid the binder across the table. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Take it. It's out of date. The idea isn't."</span> Then she was a charge nurse again. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Now. Your vendor. When's the mock code?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Require a live failover test before go-live, timed, with the units on paper.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Maria tells her what she'll ask for on Monday. Before go-live, Tributary fails its platform over to its second region for real, on a Saturday, with Nate's team watching and a clock running against Cascade's recovery targets. At the same hour, the Wyoming units run their downtime procedures on paper, start to finish. Irene listens the way she used to listen to a plan for a sick patient, and then she nods once. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"That's a mock code."</span> She pours them both more coffee. It's gone cold. Neither of them gets up to warm it.`,
          why:`A recovery plan is a guess until you've run it. Require a live failover test before go-live, timed against the RTO and RPO, while there's still time to fix what breaks. Have the units practice their downtime procedures at the same hour, because the people are part of the plan too.`},
        {t:`Accept a walk-through of the plan now, and the vendor's live test in January.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Tributary's engineers walk Nate's team through the plan in June, page by page, and it sounds good. When Maria tells Irene about it on the phone, Irene asks one question. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Did anybody's hands move?"</span> Nobody's had.`,
          why:`A tabletop walk-through is a fair start. It finds gaps in the paper. It can't prove the failover works, and a January test leaves three months of patients depending on a guess. Test it live before go-live.`},
        {t:`Approve the plan as written, since it's thorough and a test would slow the build.`, s:0, flag:`nodrtest`, d:{trust:-3,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`Irene doesn't argue. She closes the binder and looks out the window at the crab apple for a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"Okay,"</span> she says. In fourteen years Maria never heard Irene say okay and mean it. On Monday Nate signs the design review under protest, in writing, and keeps a copy.`,
          why:`A recovery plan nobody has run is a hope with page numbers. A thorough document isn't evidence. Test failover before go-live, against the targets, while a failure is still cheap.`},
        {t:`Have Marcus build a second copy of every feed in Relay, so the test won't matter.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Marcus could do it in about four months of nights. Then Cascade would have two copies of every feed, neither one tested, and one more thing only Marcus understands. Irene raises an eyebrow. <span class="said" data-who="Irene">"So two binders nobody's opened."</span>`,
          why:`A second copy doesn't prove either one works. It adds cost and another single point of failure. Make the vendor show the recovery its contract promises.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s2`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB4`, title:`Whose minutes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, May 18, at 7:30, Nate came to Ochoa's office with Maria and a one-page draft. One row for every system in One Chart: back within four hours, lose no more than one. He'd brought it from his last hospital.`,
        `Glenn's analyst had already priced the other way, the one Aisha had asked for in an email: every system on hot standby, back in minutes, nothing lost. $3.1 million over five years.`,
        `Ochoa read both and slid them across the table to Maria. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Neither one of these came from a nurse."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use Corvane's recommended recovery targets, since Corvane built the system.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Corvane's targets are reasonable and generic. They treat the data warehouse like the eMAR, and they don't know Rawlins has one doctor at night.`,
          why:`A vendor's recommendation is a fair starting point. Your owners still have to set targets for your patients and your operation.`},
        {t:`Put every system on hot standby, since any lost data could hurt a patient.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:0},
          after:`Glenn reads the $3.1 million and asks which patient is harmed if the data warehouse is down for a day. Nobody can name one. The request comes back cut in half, at random.`,
          why:`Zero downtime for everything spends money where it doesn't protect anyone. Tier the targets by impact, so the money goes where an hour matters.`},
        {t:`Have each system's owner set its targets in a business impact analysis.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Three weeks of short interviews. Maria and Nate ask each owner the same questions. What happens to patients and the operation at fifteen minutes down? At an hour? At a day? The CNOs answer for the chart, the lab director for results, the revenue cycle director for billing, the analytics director for the warehouse. Gail answers for Rawlins without being asked. The targets come back different for every system, and each owner signs their own row on May 28. Hot standby goes only to the chart, where an hour matters. The cost drops to $1.4 million. Glenn checks the math and sends back one word. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Approved."</span>`,
          why:`Recovery targets are business decisions. A business impact analysis asks the people who own each process what an outage costs over time, and the owners set the RTO and RPO. IT designs to meet them, and spends the most where patients need it most.`},
        {t:`Let Nate's team set every target, since recovering systems is IT's job.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Nate's team sets them carefully, and they're IT's guesses. The lab director learns her RTO from an email in June and calls it fiction.`,
          why:`IT is the custodian, not the owner. The people who carry the impact set the targets, and IT builds to them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s3`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E7`, title:`One road`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, June 3, the owners' targets were taped to Maria's wall, one page, every row signed.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="One Chart recovery targets">Corvane EHR. Back within one hour. Lose no more than fifteen minutes.<br>Tributary transfer feeds. Back within four hours. Lose nothing. Every message delivered or replayed.<br>Lab system. Back within four hours. Lose no more than fifteen minutes.<br>Billing. Back within twenty-four hours. Lose no more than one hour.<br>Patient portal. Back within twenty-four hours. Lose no more than four hours.<br>Data warehouse. Back within seventy-two hours. Lose no more than twenty-four hours.</prop>`,
        `Nate stood in front of it with a network map of Wyoming. Rawlins reached Cascade over one circuit from one carrier, buried along the interstate. When a road crew cut it in February, Rawlins lost its lab feeds and the exchange for eleven hours, and MedaLink, on a server in the hospital's basement, kept running. After October, Rawlins' chart would live in Fort Collins, three hours away by road.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Nate">"The data center can be perfect,"</span> Nate said. <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"If the line's cut, Rawlins can't reach it. The carrier's contract says four hours to repair. In February it took eleven."</span> Gail was on the speaker from Rawlins. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Eleven hours with a chart I can't open is a long night."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Add a backup circuit from the same carrier, and test it every quarter.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The backup circuit goes into the same trench as the first one. The next road crew can cut both with one bucket.`,
          why:`A second circuit is right. Sharing a carrier and a path means one event takes out both. Redundancy has to be independent.`},
        {t:`Accept the risk, since the carrier's contract requires four-hour repairs.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The contract pays a credit when a repair runs long. In February the credit was $412. It didn't help the nurse at 3 AM.`,
          why:`A service credit pays the hospital back. It doesn't keep patients safe during the outage. Design the network so care continues when one link fails.`},
        {t:`Keep MedaLink running in the Rawlins basement as a fallback, for good.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`MedaLink keeps running after its vendor stops patching it. By spring it's the least secure computer in Wyoming, holding a copy of the chart nobody updates.`,
          why:`An unsupported system isn't a fallback. It's a growing security risk full of stale data. Build a supported read-only view instead.`},
        {t:`Add a second circuit on a separate path, plus a read-only downtime view.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nate finds a second carrier with fiber along the rail line, a different trench in a different right of way, and the routers switch between the two on their own. On every Rawlins unit, a downtime computer on battery, with its own printer, keeps a read-only copy of the census, active medications, allergies, and the last day of results, refreshed every fifteen minutes. Gail asks who tests it. Nate says the units do, every quarter, by pulling the plug. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"On purpose?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Nate">"That's the only test that counts."</span>`,
          why:`Look for single points of failure along the whole path, not just in the data center. Redundant circuits only help when they can't fail together, so use separate carriers and separate routes. A read-only downtime view keeps critical information on the unit when the network can't reach the chart.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s4`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB6`, title:`Admit time`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, June 8, Kiran brought Maria a mapping problem with three answers. MedaLink at Laramie stamped admit time when a bed was assigned. Cheyenne stamped it when the patient reached the unit. Corvane stamped it when the doctor signed the admit order. Three different moments, sometimes six hours apart, all called admit time.`,
        `Every report that used it would jump at go-live: length of stay, ED boarding, the transfer numbers in Glenn's business case. Aisha wanted Corvane's meaning. Gail wanted Cheyenne's, because that was when her nurses took over. Kiran had a fourth idea, and a spreadsheet.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let Kiran choose the definition that maps cleanest, and document it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Kiran picks Corvane's, because it maps with the fewest rules, and he documents it well. Gail finds out from a report in November that her nurses' hour is gone.`,
          why:`Writing it down is right. What the data means is a business decision, though. The analyst builds what the owner decides.`},
        {t:`Take it to data governance: the owner defines, the steward records.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`The data governance council meets on the 17th. Patient flow data belongs to the CNOs, and they settle it in one meeting with Aisha in the room. Admit time is when the admit order is signed. Arrival on the unit gets its own field, so Gail keeps her hour. The HIM director, the steward, writes both into the data dictionary with an owner, a definition, and the date it changed. Converted records keep their old meaning in a note, and every trend report gets a marked line at October. Kiran reads the entry and lets out a breath. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"So it isn't mine to decide."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Nope. It's yours to build."</span>`,
          why:`Data governance gives each data element an owner who defines it, a steward who keeps the definition and its quality, and a custodian who stores and protects it. Definitions live in the data dictionary. When one changes, mark the break, so nobody compares two meanings as if they were one.`},
        {t:`Keep each hospital's own meaning of it, so none of their reports change.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Every hospital's own reports stay the same. The system's reports now add three different things together and call the total admit time.`,
          why:`One system needs one definition per data element, recorded in a data dictionary. Local meanings make system-wide reports wrong.`},
        {t:`Use Corvane's meaning, and convert all of the history without a note.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`It converts cleanly. In January, Wyoming's length of stay seems to rise four hours overnight, and the board quality committee asks what went wrong on the floors.`,
          why:`Changing a definition without marking it makes a data change look like a change in care. Record the change and mark the series.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s5`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB6`, title:`Two charts`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, June 15, Corvane's conversion lead brought the first test load of the master patient index. Of 186,000 Wyoming patients, 141,000 matched someone already in Corvane with near certainty. Another 6,200 were possible matches: the same name and birthday at a different address, or the same everything with one digit off.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="man">"Reviewing 6,200 by hand takes two months,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"We can lower the threshold and merge them automatically. Most of them are the same person."</span>`,
        `Most. Maria thought about a chart split in two, half a patient's history in a record nobody would think to open. It was a missing page with a different name.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Lower the threshold and merge all 6,200 automatically, to hold the date.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`It holds the date. In testing, a Rawlins rancher's chart shows his son's blood thinner. HIM spends the fall pulling records apart, which is much harder than merging them.`,
          why:`An overlay, two people in one chart, is one of the most dangerous data errors there is. Never lower a matching threshold to save time.`},
        {t:`Start every Wyoming patient with a brand new record, so nothing mismerges.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Nothing merges wrongly, and nothing merges at all. Every Wyoming patient ever seen in Greeley now has two charts, and neither one shows the other.`,
          why:`New records for everyone guarantee duplicates. Use the index to link the same person, and review what's uncertain.`},
        {t:`Link only the sure matches, and have HIM review the 6,200 before go-live.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`The HIM director, the index's steward, puts three trained reviewers on it, one paid for by Corvane under the statement of work. They finish in seven weeks. Most pairs are the same person, as promised. Forty-one aren't: twins, a father and son in Rawlins with the same name at the same ranch address, two women in Laramie with the same birthday and the same married name. The lower threshold would have put each pair's medications and allergies in one chart. After the load the duplicate rate is 0.4 percent, and the HIM director sets a target to keep it under one.`,
          why:`Protect the master patient index. Link automatically only the matches you're sure of, and send the uncertain middle to trained HIM review. A duplicate splits a history, and an overlay mixes two people's. Measure the duplicate rate before and after.`},
        {t:`Convert them all as separate records, and let HIM merge after go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Nobody is merged wrongly. At go-live, 6,200 patients have two charts, and the Laramie ED keeps finding allergies in the other one.`,
          why:`Avoiding bad merges is right. Going live with known duplicates splits histories at the moment clinicians need them. Clean up before conversion.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C6`, title:`Thirty-four`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, June 22, the Clinical Informatics Council met with three new chairs at the table, one for each Wyoming hospital. Kiran's thirty-four Wyoming order sets were on the screen beside Corvane's system sets, the ones the other eight hospitals used.`,
        `Walt had marked his up in red. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Mine's two clicks shorter than yours,"</span> he told the system's chief of surgery, over the speaker from Laramie. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I'll trade you."</span>`,
        `Kiran had already written it up. His team would own all of them, system and Wyoming, and keep them current. He looked like he hadn't slept, and he probably hadn't. At the break Maria asked him when he'd done it. He shrugged.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, wry, a little proud.">"When I was a pharmacy tech, I was the one they called at two in the morning, because I knew where everything was,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, wry, a little proud.">"I liked being that guy. I guess I still do."</span> Maria knew exactly what that felt like, which was the problem.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Kiran's team own and update every order set, so they stay consistent.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Kiran says yes, of course. By August he's the only person who knows why any order set says what it says, and he's answering pages about them at night.`,
          why:`IT can't own clinical content. Clinicians decide what's in it and keep it current. And one analyst owning everything is a single point of failure with a pulse.`},
        {t:`Merge into one set each, with a clinical owner, a review date, and versions.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The council takes them one at a time over four meetings. Where Wyoming's set is better, it wins: Walt's discharge set beats the system's by two clicks and becomes everyone's. Every set gets a physician or pharmacist owner, a review date two years out or sooner when the evidence changes, a version number, and a form for requesting changes. Usage reports go to the council every quarter, and sets nobody opens get retired. Kiran builds them. He doesn't own them. Aisha puts the sepsis alert on the same schedule. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Everything gets a birthday,"</span> she says, <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"and a doctor who has to show up to it."</span>`,
          why:`Clinical content stays safe through a governed process: clinical owners, review cycles, version control, a way to request changes, and usage data to retire what nobody uses. IT builds the content. Clinicians own it.`},
        {t:`Let Wyoming keep its own sets for a year, then merge them with the system's.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Two versions of everything go live in October. In January a Cheyenne doctor covering Laramie orders from the wrong one, and the council spends spring merging what it could have merged in June.`,
          why:`Listening to Wyoming is right. Running parallel content after go-live doubles the upkeep and trips up clinicians who cover more than one site. Merge with Wyoming at the table now.`},
        {t:`Let each physician build personal favorites, so nobody has to compromise.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`By the first upgrade there are four hundred personal sets, and nobody knows which ones have the new heparin protocol.`,
          why:`Personal order sets drift away from current evidence and can't be maintained. Standard content with clinical owners is safer.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s7`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB1`, title:`The pumps`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, June 29, Maria went down to the Relay room, where Marcus had a whiteboard covered in boxes and two junior analysts covered in questions. One Chart would bring smart infusion pumps to Laramie and Rawlins. Corvane would send the ordered rate to the pump, and the pump would send back what it gave.`,
        ((s.flags.blamedmarcus||G.blamedmarcus)
          ?`Marcus taught every afternoon now, and he was good at it. He didn't look up when she came in. Since the readout in Laramie, he talked to her mostly in writing, and she'd learned not to push.`
          :`Marcus taught every afternoon now. He pointed her to a chair without stopping. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"Sit. You're about to learn something too."</span>`),
        `The biomed manager from Laramie was on the phone. Her new pumps were in boxes, and Corvane's build team wanted them on the hospital's main network next week, plugged in like everything else, so testing could start.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put the pumps on the main hospital network next week, so testing can start.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The pumps go onto the network beside the cafeteria registers. Nate finds them in a scan and asks why a device running a ten-year-old operating system can talk to the payroll server.`,
          why:`Medical devices often run old software that can't be patched quickly. Put them on their own network segment, and plan the integration with biomed and pharmacy.`},
        {t:`Design it with biomed, pharmacy, and nursing on a device network, then validate.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:6},
          after:`Marcus runs it as a lesson. His two analysts write the test plan while he watches: every drug in the library, every concentration, the ordered rate going out and the given volume coming back. Pharmacy owns the drug library and signs it. Biomed puts the pumps on their own segment of the network, where Nate can watch them. Nursing picks twenty real scenarios from the Laramie floor. In the first test the pump reads a heparin rate in units an hour that Corvane sent in milliliters, and one of the analysts catches it before Marcus does. He doesn't say anything. He writes her name on the whiteboard, under his.`,
          why:`Medical device integration takes clinical engineering, pharmacy, nursing, and IT together. Segment the devices on their own network, have pharmacy own the drug library, and validate every data flow with real scenarios before a nurse depends on it.`},
        {t:`Connect the pumps at go-live, and validate them during the first live week.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The pumps go live with everything else. In the first week a nurse finds the units don't match on a heparin drip, at the bedside, which is where the testing happened.`,
          why:`Validating in production means the first real test is on a patient. Test device integration before go-live.`},
        {t:`Run the pumps standalone at go-live, and connect them once it's settled.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It's safe enough, and it's what Wyoming does today. It also puts off the part of One Chart that stops rate errors, and nobody puts a date on once it's settled.`,
          why:`Holding integration back avoids go-live risk, which is fair. It also delays a safety benefit. Plan it, test it, and give it a date.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s8`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E5`, title:`The cattle guard`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, July 7, Gail called about the portal. Fourteen percent of Rawlins patients had signed up for MedaLink's, and most of them used it to pay bills. Corvane's portal did more: results, medication lists, messages to the care team, video visits. It also let patients connect apps of their own choosing to their record through a FHIR interface, the kind federal rules now expect.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Half my patients lose signal at the cattle guard,"</span> Gail said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"The other half have a grandkid who sets up their phone at Christmas. Don't tell me about apps."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Mail every Wyoming patient a letter about the new portal after go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The letter goes out in November. Sign-ups rise from fourteen percent to seventeen, mostly people who were already on the old portal.`,
          why:`Telling patients is a start. Enrollment at the point of care, with help, is what gets a portal used.`},
        {t:`Start Wyoming patients on the new portal fresh, without the old history.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`New portal, empty chart. Patients log in, see nothing from before October, and log out.`,
          why:`A portal without the patient's history gives them little reason to use it. Bring the data over.`},
        {t:`Bring their history over, and sign up patients and caregivers at discharge.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Two years of MedaLink results and medication lists convert into Corvane, so the portal has something in it on day one. Before discharge, a nurse or a unit clerk signs the patient up on the patient's own phone, or on a caregiver's, with proxy access the patient approves. Patients who'd rather use an app can connect one. Patients with no signal can get results by phone. Gail picks the measures herself: sign-ups, results viewed, and how many heart failure patients message about their weight before they come back through her ED. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Fine. Christmas comes early."</span>`,
          why:`Portals and patient apps improve outcomes when patients actually use them. That takes history worth seeing, sign-up at the point of care, proxy access for caregivers, and options for people with poor connectivity. Measure use, not accounts.`},
        {t:`Hold the portal back a year after go-live, so staff have time to settle.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`For a year, three hospitals have no results online. The Corvane hospitals' patients do, and Wyoming's notice.`,
          why:`Delaying patient access widens the gap between sites and can run against patient access rules. Build enrollment into go-live.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s9`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SB5`, title:`The patch`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, July 13, Aisha forwarded a pitch with a heart in the subject line. Meadowlark, a startup in Boulder, made a patch that heart failure patients wore at home. It sent heart rhythm, breathing, and movement to the cloud, and an AI model scored each patient's risk of coming back to the hospital. The company offered a free year for a hundred patients.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Wyoming readmits heart failure at almost twice our rate,"</span> Aisha said. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"This is exactly what we should be doing. Let's start at Rawlins in September."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Start the free pilot at Rawlins in September, and judge it after a year.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The patches go out in September, a month before go-live. Alerts arrive as emailed PDFs in a shared inbox nobody watches on weekends. By November nobody can say whether it's helping.`,
          why:`Piloting is right. A pilot with no evaluation criteria, no owner for the alerts, and no baseline can't tell you anything.`},
        {t:`Run it through the technology intake: evidence, FDA status, fit, and a pilot.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`The intake group meets the next week: Aisha, a heart failure nurse from Cheyenne, Nate, Joan, Marcus, and Glenn's analyst. The answers go on one page. The patch is cleared by the FDA as a monitor. The risk score isn't cleared for anything yet, and its study was at two city hospitals. Its data would arrive as a PDF, not through FHIR. Nobody at Cascade watches alerts at night. After the free year, it's $400 a patient a month. They plan a pilot of forty patients for February, after go-live, with a nurse watching alerts on days, a FHIR feed, a matched baseline, and a rule for stopping early. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"You're slower than I want."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"And if it works, we'll know why."</span>`,
          why:`Evaluate emerging technology before you adopt it: the evidence in patients like yours, regulatory status, standards-based integration, who acts on the output and when, privacy and security terms, cost after the pilot, and whether it works where your patients live. Then pilot it with measures and a stop rule set in advance. That intake path is how an organization brings trends in on purpose instead of by accident.`},
        {t:`Buy it for all three hospitals now, since the readmission gap is so wide.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The contract is signed in August. In December Cascade learns the model was built on city patients, and it flags most of Rawlins as high risk because they live far from a hospital.`,
          why:`A wide gap makes a promising tool tempting. Buying before you've checked the evidence and the fit risks money and patients' trust. Pilot first.`},
        {t:`Turn down anything new until One Chart has been live for at least a year.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`It keeps the team focused. It also means Wyoming's heart failure patients wait two years for anything, and Aisha takes the pitch to the board herself.`,
          why:`Saying no to everything isn't governance. A process to evaluate and schedule new technology lets you say not yet, with a date.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s10`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E6`, title:`Row 47`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, July 16, Corvane's cutover plan listed the applications that would move with the three hospitals. There were nineteen, all clinical. Marcus had his own list, built from every interface that touched MedaLink. It had sixty-three.`,
        `Row 47 was Lumen, the discharge forecasting tool that read every admission and discharge. If its MedaLink feed went quiet in October, Lumen would forecast three empty hospitals. Most of the rows after it belonged to departments nobody had asked: a claims scrubber, a staffing system, the dietary feed, a cancer registry, the data warehouse.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Sort all sixty-three by family, and give each an owner and a cutover plan.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`By the end of the month every row has a family and an owner. Clinical, twenty-six. Administrative, eleven. Financial, fourteen. Consumer, two. Business intelligence, ten, Lumen among them. Each owner picks a fate: move it, replace it with Corvane, retire it, or reconnect it through Tributary. The revenue cycle director finds two claims tools nobody in IT knew existed, and the cancer registrar finds out her feed was about to stop.`,
          why:`Know every application in your environment and which family it belongs to: clinical, administrative, financial, consumer, or business intelligence. A cutover that plans only the clinical systems breaks billing, reporting, and patient tools without warning. Every application needs an owner and a decision.`},
        {t:`Cut over the nineteen clinical ones, and handle the rest after go-live.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The clinical systems cut over cleanly. On Monday the claims scrubber has nothing to scrub, and Lumen forecasts Rawlins at zero patients.`,
          why:`Clinical first is a fair priority. Leaving the rest for later means they break at go-live. Plan every family.`},
        {t:`Retire every application Corvane can replace, without asking departments.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Eleven applications are set to go dark. Three of them hold records the hospitals are required to keep.`,
          why:`Retiring an application is its owner's decision, and it needs a plan for the data. Ask before you turn things off.`},
        {t:`Ask the departments to tell IT what else they need, sometime by September.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Some departments answer. The ones that don't are the ones that break.`,
          why:`IT holds the interface list, so IT leads the inventory. Waiting for departments to volunteer misses the systems nobody thinks about.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s11`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L3`, title:`Five years out`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, July 20, at 7:30, Ochoa had the capital plan open. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Glenn wants five years of what One Chart will need,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Bandwidth, storage, licenses, people. Kiran sent me this."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Five years,"</span> he said. He wrote 2032 at the top of his legal pad and looked at it longer than the number needed. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, far off, plain.">"Build it for whoever's reading it then."</span>`,
        `Kiran's sheet took last year's numbers and added ten percent a year to every line. It was neat, and it had cost him a weekend. Maria thought about the summer. A second circuit to Rawlins. Patients' apps reading the chart. A patch that might send heart rhythms from a hundred ranches. None of it was in last year's numbers.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep Kiran's ten percent a year, and add a twenty percent safety margin.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`The margin makes the totals bigger, and no more right. The new video and app traffic to Rawlins alone would use up the margin in year two.`,
          why:`A trend plus a margin assumes the future looks like the past. New services change the shape of demand, so forecast from the drivers.`},
        {t:`Ask each vendor what Cascade will need, and add up the vendors' numbers.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Each vendor's estimate is generous about its own product and silent about everyone else's. None of them includes the circuit to Rawlins.`,
          why:`Vendors can inform a forecast, but they don't know your strategy. The organization forecasts its own needs.`},
        {t:`Buy ten years of capacity now, so that nobody has to forecast this again.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:0},
          after:`Glenn asks why Cascade should pay in 2027 for storage that will cost far less by the time it's needed. Nobody has an answer he likes.`,
          why:`Overbuying ties up capital in technology that will age. Forecast, then add capacity in stages.`},
        {t:`Build the forecast from its drivers: volumes, new services, users, plans.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria and Kiran rebuild it Wednesday and Thursday, and he leaves at five both nights. Every line starts with what drives it. Bandwidth: Rawlins' chart in Fort Collins, video visits, patient apps, and a possible patch program, each with its own estimate. Storage: imaging growth and seven years of converted records. Messages through Tributary: transfers, the exchange, and apps. People: two analysts who can read FHIR, which Marcus is already building. Each line names the plan it serves and gets redone every year. Ochoa reads every line. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"First forecast I've seen that tells me why."</span>`,
          why:`Forecast technical and information needs from their drivers: volumes, new services, users, regulations, and the strategic plan. Tie each line to the plan it serves, and revisit it every year. Last year plus a percentage misses everything that's new.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f8s12`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E5`, title:`Nights from Greeley`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, July 27, Gail drove down from Rawlins for the design sign-off and stayed after. She'd been thinking about nights. Rawlins had one doctor in the ED after seven and no critical care nurse at all, so its sickest patients rode three hours to Greeley.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Gail">"The ones who can't wait three hours are the ones I worry about,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Greeley has ICU nurses awake all night. I want them watching my two sickest beds."</span>`,
        `Maria knew who'd be awake. Bev Hart, at the charge desk where Irene used to sit, where the fax machine used to be.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put cameras in the two Rawlins rooms now, and write the rules as it runs.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The cameras go up in August. On the first night a Greeley nurse sees a pressure falling and doesn't know whether she's supposed to call the Rawlins doctor or the Rawlins nurse. She calls both. Neither one expected her.`,
          why:`The technology is the easy part. Roles, escalation, and measures have to be designed before the first night.`},
        {t:`Keep sending Rawlins' sickest patients to Greeley, since that works today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Most of them make it fine. Some don't do well on three hours of highway, and those are the ones Gail meant.`,
          why:`Transfer is sometimes right. A remote ICU can let a small hospital keep patients who'd do better staying, and get help to the ones who can't wait.`},
        {t:`Design a remote ICU pilot with both units: roles, escalation, measures.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Bev comes to the second meeting. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Kid, I'm too old for a camera."</span> Then she asks who calls whom, and when, and who can order what, which is how Maria knows she's in. The design fits on one page. Greeley ICU nurses watch two Rawlins beds by video and monitor feed from seven at night to seven in the morning, with the same chart open. The Rawlins nurse stays the patient's nurse. Escalation goes to the Rawlins doctor first, then a Greeley intensivist. Joan and legal check licensing across the state line, and consent. The measures are set before the first night: transfers avoided, time to rescue, and a survey of both units' nurses. It starts in February, after go-live. Bev reads the page. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Irene would've liked this."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"She will. I'm telling her Saturday."</span>`,
          why:`Telehealth improves outcomes when it's designed as care, not hardware: clear roles at both ends, escalation paths, licensing and consent checked, one shared record, and outcome measures set before it starts.`},
        {t:`Wait until One Chart has been live a year before any kind of telehealth.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`It keeps One Chart's first year clean. Rawlins nights stay the same for two more winters.`,
          why:`A big project doesn't freeze every other improvement. Design the telehealth now and schedule it after go-live.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f8s2`, title:`The cheapest design that works`, setup:[`Nate priced four recovery designs, cheapest first. Design 1, nightly backups restored onto new hardware: back in 72 hours, loses up to 24 hours of data. Design 2, a warm standby with data copied every hour: back in 8 hours, loses up to 1 hour. Design 3, a warm standby with data copied every 15 minutes: back in 4 hours, loses up to 15 minutes. Design 4, a hot standby with a live copy and automatic failover: back in 15 minutes, loses nothing.`,`The owners' targets are in the table. A design meets a system's targets only if it's back at least as fast as the RTO and loses no more than the RPO. For each system, enter the number of the cheapest design that meets both.`],
      table:[[`System`,`RTO`,`RPO`],[`Corvane EHR`,`1 hr`,`15 min`],[`Lab system`,`4 hr`,`15 min`],[`Billing`,`24 hr`,`1 hr`],[`Patient portal`,`24 hr`,`4 hr`],[`Data warehouse`,`72 hr`,`24 hr`]],
      fields:[{label:`Data warehouse, design number`, answer:1, tol:0},{label:`Patient portal, design number`, answer:2, tol:0},{label:`Lab system, design number`, answer:3, tol:0},{label:`Corvane EHR, design number`, answer:4, tol:0},{label:`Most lab data design 2 could lose, minutes`, answer:60, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Check both targets for each system, cheapest design first, and stop at the first one that passes both.`,`Data warehouse, 72 hours and 24 hours. Design 1 is back in 72 hours and loses up to 24, so it meets both. Design 1.`,`Patient portal, 24 hours and 4 hours. Design 1 takes 72 hours, too slow. Design 2 is back in 8 and loses up to 1. Design 2. Billing works the same way at 24 hours and 1 hour: design 2 loses up to exactly 1 hour, which meets it.`,`Lab system, 4 hours and 15 minutes. Design 2 takes 8 hours and can lose 60 minutes, so it fails both. Design 3 is back in 4 hours and loses up to 15 minutes. Design 3.`,`Corvane EHR, 1 hour and 15 minutes. Design 3 takes 4 hours, too slow. Design 4 is back in 15 minutes. Design 4. That's why hot standby went only to the chart.`,`Design 2 copies data once an hour, so a failure just before the next copy loses up to 60 minutes. That's four times the lab's 15-minute RPO: 60 / 15 = 4.`] },
    { after:`f8s3`, title:`Availability in minutes`, setup:[`A 30-day month has 30 x 24 x 60 = 43,200 minutes. Allowed downtime = (1 minus availability) x minutes in the period.`,`Rawlins' carrier promises 99.5 percent availability on its circuit. Tributary's contract, signed in May, promises 99.9 percent. Nate's second circuit, on a separate path, also promises 99.5 percent. If the two circuits fail independently, Rawlins loses its connection only when both are down at once. The chance of that is one circuit's down share times the other's.`,`Round percents to four decimals and minutes to two.`],
      fields:[{label:`Downtime allowed on one 99.5 percent circuit, minutes a month`, answer:216, tol:0.1},{label:`Downtime allowed at 99.9 percent, minutes a month`, answer:43.2, tol:0.1},{label:`Availability of two independent 99.5 percent circuits, percent`, answer:99.9975, tol:0.0001},{label:`Expected minutes a month with both circuits down`, answer:1.08, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`One circuit: (1 minus 0.995) x 43,200 = 0.005 x 43,200 = 216 minutes, or 3.6 hours a month.`,`Tributary: (1 minus 0.999) x 43,200 = 0.001 x 43,200 = 43.2 minutes a month.`,`Two circuits: each is down 0.005 of the time. Both at once: 0.005 x 0.005 = 0.000025. Availability = 1 minus 0.000025 = 0.999975, or 99.9975 percent.`,`Minutes with both down: 0.000025 x 43,200 = 1.08 minutes a month.`,`The math only holds if the circuits can't fail together. Two circuits in the same trench share one backhoe, and together they're barely better than one. Independence is the whole design.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f8s4`, title:`Owner, steward, or custodian?`, intro:`Data governance gives each job to one role. Pick the role that fits.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Approves the official definition of admit time for nursing reports across the system.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:0, why:`The owner is the business leader accountable for the data. The owner decides what it means and who may use it.`},
      {prompt:`Keeps the data dictionary current and fixes errors in a data element's definition.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:1, why:`The steward maintains definitions and data quality day to day, for the owner.`},
      {prompt:`Runs the nightly backups and grants the access the owner has approved.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:2, why:`The custodian, usually IT, stores and protects the data and carries out the owner's access decisions.`},
      {prompt:`A charge nurse who reads length of stay on a monthly report.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:3, why:`A user works with the data under the access the owner approved.`},
      {prompt:`An HIM analyst who reviews possible duplicate patient records and corrects the index.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:1, why:`HIM staff act as stewards of the master patient index, keeping its quality high.`},
      {prompt:`A cloud vendor that stores and encrypts the data under its contract.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:2, why:`A vendor that holds and protects data for you is a custodian. The organization still owns the data.`},
      {prompt:`Decides how long ICU quality data is kept and who is allowed to see it.`, choices:[`Data owner`,`Data steward`,`Data custodian`,`Data user`], answer:0, why:`Retention and access are the owner's decisions, within policy and the law.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A system's owners agree it must be running again within one hour of a failure. Which term names that target?`, opts:[`Recovery time objective`,`Recovery point objective`,`Mean time to repair`,`Service availability`], a:0, why:`The recovery time objective is how fast a system must be restored. The recovery point objective is how much data can be lost. Mean time to repair is a measured average, not a target the owners set.`, task:`SB4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Owners set a 15-minute recovery point objective for the lab system. Its backups run every four hours. What does that mean?`, opts:[`The lab can't be restored within 15 minutes`,`The backups meet the target with time to spare`,`Up to four hours of results could be lost`,`The recovery time objective must be raised`], a:2, why:`The RPO is the most data you can lose, counted back from the failure. Backups every four hours mean a failure just before the next one loses up to 240 minutes, sixteen times the 15-minute target. The RPO says nothing about restore time, which is the RTO.`, task:`SB4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A cloud vendor's disaster recovery plan is detailed but has never been run. Go-live is five months away. What should the IT leader require?`, opts:[`A walk-through of the plan with the vendor`,`A timed, live failover test before go-live`,`The vendor's written promise that it works`,`A second vendor holding a copy of all data`], a:1, why:`A plan isn't proven until it's been run. A live failover test before go-live, measured against the RTO and RPO, finds what breaks while it's cheap to fix. A walk-through finds gaps on paper, and a promise proves nothing.`, task:`SB4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which recovery site is fully equipped, with current data, and ready to take over within minutes to hours?`, opts:[`A cold site`,`A warm site`,`An off-site backup`,`A hot site`], a:3, why:`A hot site is fully equipped with current data and takes over in minutes to hours; a mirrored site adds a live copy with automatic failover. A warm site is partly equipped and needs data restored, so hours to days. A cold site is space, power, and network only, days to weeks.`, task:`SB4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A circuit's contract promises 99.5 percent availability in a 30-day month. How much downtime does that allow?`, opts:[`43.2 minutes`,`2.16 minutes`,`216 minutes`,`720 minutes`], a:2, why:`A 30-day month has 43,200 minutes. (1 minus 0.995) x 43,200 = 0.005 x 43,200 = 216 minutes. 43.2 minutes is what 99.9 percent allows, and 720 minutes treats 0.5 percent as half a day.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A rural hospital will reach its EHR over one network circuit after go-live. Which design BEST protects care if the circuit is cut?`, opts:[`A second circuit on a separate path, and a downtime view`,`A second circuit from the same carrier in the same trench`,`A carrier contract promising repairs within four hours`,`A faster circuit with twice the bandwidth of the current one`], a:0, why:`Redundancy only works when the paths can't fail together, so the second circuit needs its own route. A read-only downtime view keeps critical data on the unit if both fail. A repair promise and more bandwidth don't help during a cut.`, task:`E7`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Three hospitals stamp "admit time" at different moments. Who should decide the definition the whole system will use?`, opts:[`The analyst mapping the data`,`The EHR vendor's build team`,`The database administrator`,`The data owner, via governance`], a:3, why:`The business owner defines a data element through data governance, and the steward records it in the data dictionary. The analyst, the vendor, and the database administrator build and protect what the owner decides.`, task:`SB6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An EHR conversion finds thousands of possible duplicate patient matches. Which approach is BEST before go-live?`, opts:[`Auto-merge all possible matches to hold the date`,`Link sure matches, and review the uncertain ones`,`Create new records for every converted patient`,`Leave the duplicates for HIM to fix after go-live`], a:1, why:`Automatic linking is for matches you're sure of. Trained HIM review of the uncertain middle prevents overlays, which mix two people in one chart, and cleaning up before go-live keeps histories whole when clinicians need them.`, task:`SB6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A tool reads admission and census feeds to predict which patients will go home tomorrow. Which application family does it belong to?`, opts:[`Clinical documentation`,`Revenue cycle billing`,`Business intelligence`,`Patient engagement`], a:2, why:`A tool that analyzes operational data to forecast and inform decisions is business intelligence. It doesn't document care, bill for it, or serve patients directly.`, task:`E6`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Requests for new technology reach IT from every direction. What should a health system set up FIRST to bring useful trends in?`, opts:[`One intake process with criteria and governance`,`A rule that IT approves every request it receives`,`A fund each department can spend on new tools`,`A freeze on new technology until the EHR is live`], a:0, why:`A single governed intake screens requests against the strategy, evaluates them, pilots what passes with measures, and then scales or stops them. IT alone, open spending, and a freeze each skip the evaluation.`, task:`SB3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A startup offers a free pilot of a wearable that predicts heart failure readmissions. Which question matters MOST before agreeing?`, opts:[`Whether the pilot can start before the quarter ends`,`How many other hospitals have signed up this year`,`Whether the device can carry the system's branding`,`What evidence supports it, and who acts on alerts`], a:3, why:`Evaluating an emerging technology starts with evidence in patients like yours, its regulatory status, and the workflow for acting on what it finds. Timing, popularity, and branding don't tell you whether it's safe or useful.`, task:`SB5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A hospital will connect smart infusion pumps to its EHR. Which approach is BEST?`, opts:[`Put the pumps on the main network so they work like PCs`,`Design it with biomed and pharmacy, then validate it`,`Connect them at go-live and validate in the first week`,`Let the pump vendor set up the drug library on its own`], a:1, why:`Device integration takes clinical engineering, pharmacy, nursing, and IT together, with the pumps on their own network segment and every data flow validated before use. Pharmacy owns the drug library, and validation belongs before go-live.`, task:`SB1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A small rural hospital has no critical care nurse at night. Which technology trend BEST helps it care for sicker patients safely?`, opts:[`A patient portal with secure messaging`,`A data warehouse for quality reporting`,`Remote ICU monitoring by video from a hub`,`Self-service kiosks for patient check-in`], a:2, why:`Remote ICU monitoring lets critical care nurses and physicians at a hub watch and help with patients at a small hospital, which can catch decline earlier and avoid some transfers. The others don't reach the bedside at night.`, task:`E5`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`After go-live, which arrangement BEST keeps order sets current and safe across a health system?`, opts:[`Each physician keeps a personal set of favorites`,`The analyst team updates order sets as asked`,`Each hospital builds and keeps its own versions`,`Clinical owners review each on a set schedule`], a:3, why:`Ongoing content governance gives every order set a clinical owner, a review cycle, version control, and usage data. Analysts build the content, and clinicians own it. Personal and local versions drift from the evidence.`, task:`C6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`An IT leader must forecast network and storage needs for the next five years. What should the forecast be built on?`, opts:[`Last year's use plus a flat ten percent a year`,`Drivers like volumes, new services, and users`,`Each vendor's estimate for its own product`,`Enough capacity bought now for ten years`], a:1, why:`Forecast from the drivers: volumes, new services, users, regulations, and the strategic plan, revisited every year. A flat percentage misses what's new, vendors don't know your strategy, and overbuying ties up capital.`, task:`L3`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Friday, July 30, Ochoa signed the One Chart design at the round table in his office, with Glenn's initials already on the budget page. A hundred and forty pages: recovery targets with an owner's name on every row, a plan for the road to Rawlins, one meaning for admit time, a patient index, sixty-three applications with somebody's name beside each one.`,
    ((s.flags.nodrtest||G.nodrtest)
      ?`Nate signed the security pages last. He'd attached a page of his own, an objection to approving Tributary's recovery plan before anyone had run it, and he asked that it stay in the file. Maria said it would. She'd read every line of it. Irene's binder sat on the shelf behind her desk, where she could see it from her chair, and some days she wished she couldn't.`
      :`Nate signed the security pages last, after reading the recovery section one more time. Irene's binder sat on the shelf behind Maria's desk, the one place in the building nobody would try to file it.`),
    (s.score>=30
      ?`It had been three months of asking who owns it. Who owns the minutes, who owns a word, who owns a chart that might be two people, who owns an order set after Kiran stops building it. The answer was almost never IT, and it was almost never her. It was the people who'd carry the consequence. Her job had been to find them and get their names on the page.`
      :`It had been three months she'd want some of back. The design was signed, and most of it was sound, but some of what was sound had come from Nate's objections and Gail's stubbornness and an HIM director nobody had thanked. Ochoa would ask which parts were hers.`),
    `Sunday, August 1, Lucia found the binder. Maria had brought it home for the weekend to copy the transfer page, and left it on the kitchen table. Lucia read the cover, then the transfer page, then the cover again.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Count the pages,"</span> she read out loud. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Is this the story?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's how the story ends."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Who wrote it?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Irene. After."</span> Lucia was quiet for a second. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"So Irene knows it, and I don't."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Irene was there."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"You said when you could tell it all the way through."</span> Maria looked at the page, at the initials at the bottom. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"After go-live,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"October. All of it, start to finish. The luck too."</span> Lucia looked up. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"That's a date."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"That's a date."</span> Lucia closed the binder slowly, with both hands, the way you'd close something in a museum.`,
    `Monday, August 2, the first integrated test cycle ran on everything Corvane and Tributary had built since June. At 5:48 PM Kiran's message came in. A hundred and twenty scripts run. Eighty-nine passed. Thirty-one failed, most of them transfers. He was staying to sort them.`,
    `Maria wrote back. Go home. We'll sort them at seven. Then she opened the list.`,
    `The first failure on it was a test patient from Rawlins with twenty-three active medications, transferred to Greeley. Twenty had arrived. Nothing on the screen said there were more.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch08.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Build`
});
